---
title: The Lakeside Inn's Au Gratin Potatoes
description: Potatoes baked ahead, then simmered in half-and-half for an hour or more before garlic crouton crumbs go on.
tags: [auntie-jeanie]
course: side
icons: [potato, cheese, pan-food]
cuisine: American
source: The Lakeside Inn
adapted: true
servings: 4
---

Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C). Bake the @potatoes{1 1/2-2%lb}(680 to 900 g) for ~bake{1%hour}. Let them cool, then peel and cut into 1/2-inch cubes.

> **What's Changed** — The clipping skips the oven temperature for this bake. It also doesn't say how hot to simmer or how to tell it's done. The 400°F, the low heat and the doneness cue are our guesses. We haven't cooked it this way yet.

Line a 10-inch ovenproof #skillet{} with the potato cubes. Fill it level, and do not pile the cubes on top of one another.

Melt the @margarine{2%tbsp} in a #medium saucepan{}. Add the @half-and-half{1/4%cup}(59 ml), a dash of @salt{1%dash}, and a dash of @white pepper{1%dash}, and blend well. Pour over the potatoes.

> **The Half-and-Half** — The clipping says 1/4 cup. That seems too little for an hour of simmering. Start with 2 cups.

Simmer uncovered over low heat, barely bubbling, for ~simmer{1-1 1/2%hours}, stirring frequently. Be very careful not to let the pan boil over or overflow. Toward the end, heat the oven to 350°F (175°C).

When the half-and-half has thickened to a sauce that clings and the cubes are tender but still hold their shape, lightly dust the top with the @Parmesan cheese{1/4%cup}(grated, or an amount to coat the pan).

Mix the @cheese and garlic croutons{1/2%cup}(ground fine) with the additional @&margarine{2%tbsp}, melted, to make the crumbs moist. Spread on top.

Brown in the oven at 350°F (175°C) for about ~brown{10%minutes}.

> **Where It's From** — The Lakeside Inn in Wilbraham, Massachusetts, shared this recipe with a Springfield-area newspaper. Auntie Jeanie clipped it and made it for the family for years.
